From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 00:04:31 -0400
Subject: keep Spaces across a restart

Spaces existed only in memory, so every restart threw them away. They now ride
the session's per-window extra data as JSON, along with which one was active.

The route was already there: the vertical tab strip persists its collapsed state
and width exactly this way. SpaceModel writes through
SessionService::AddWindowExtraData on every change, session restore reads the
key back into BrowserWindowCreateParams, BrowserInitState carries it, and
SpaceModel reads it once when the window is built -- before anything can ask
which Space a tab is in.

Restore refuses to leave a window worse off than it found it. A session written
by another version, or one naming an active Space that is not in its own list,
leaves the window with its default Space rather than none; three tests cover
the round trip, five kinds of nonsense, and the unknown-active case.

Two API notes for anyone rebasing: base::Value::List and base::Value::Dict are
base::ListValue and base::DictValue in this branch, and TokenId has no
FromString -- ids parse through base::Token::FromString and FromRawToken.

Not yet: which Space each tab belonged to. Tabs come back in the active Space
until the per-tab key is written and read, which goes through
chrome::AddRestoredTab the way glic restores its own state.

Why: a feature that loses the user's arrangement on every restart is not shipped.
Removable when: the session service grows a first-class notion of tab sets.
